# 7. FAVORITES TAB

Create a dedicated Favorites destination.

Its purpose is to help students quickly find dishes they have marked as favorites within the currently eligible meal window.

It is NOT a global favorites marketplace or an unrestricted archive of every dish from breakfast, lunch, and dinner.

## 7.1 Layout

Use a compact header:

Your Favorites

Below it, display the meal context supplied by the application's existing meal-window logic.

Then display eligible favorite dishes using the same reusable compact dish cards used on Home.

Do not create a separate, visually inconsistent dish-card design for Favorites.

### Filtering requirements

Follow the existing application's meal-window eligibility logic.

Do not invent a new rule for determining which meal appears.

Do not hardcode the current meal.

Do not allow the Favorites screen to bypass meal eligibility.

If the existing application already determines which dishes are eligible, reuse that logic.

If the existing behavior does not clearly define which favorites to display while a window is closed, inspect the implementation and document the ambiguity rather than creating a new business rule.

## 7.2 Favorite removal

Tapping the heart on a favorite dish should remove it from Favorites.

The action must not trigger ordering.

If the dish is removed, update the visible list using the existing application state mechanism.

Do not remove a student's subscription or alter meal-ordering data when a favorite is changed.

## 7.3 Order action

The explicit Order button should open the existing order-confirmation dialog.

It must not directly place an order.

The confirmation dialog must preserve the existing ordering restrictions and one-dish-per-window workflow.

## 7.4 Empty state

When no eligible favorite dishes are available, show a useful empty state.

Example:

No favorite dishes for this meal yet.

Add dishes to your Favorites from Home.

If the application already supports navigating to Home, provide a suitable action to return there.

Do not display unrelated meals simply to fill the empty screen.

If the eligible window is closed, reflect the actual existing meal-window state rather than inventing a future-menu preview.

